 {"id":"fabro-meta-c9f2","title":"evidence: survive summary:high stage-renderer tail_lines cap (119 lines omitted hid the seed-work diff)","status":"open","type":"bug","priority":1,"createdAt":"2026-08-22T23:07:07.000Z","updatedAt":"2026-08-22T23:07:07.000Z","labels":["platform","painpoint-adopted"],"description":"Adopt from run 01M0NTS5XCXJ4V88M9MQWKD83G (analysis complete).\n\n## Root cause (two truncation layers)\n\nLayer 1 (OURS, working): evidence.nu self-budgets to 6.8k chars — the\noutput itself was complete and in-budget.\n\nLayer 2 (ENGINE, the killer): summary:high's per-stage section renders\ncommand output via tail_lines(...) — the reviewer's prompt received the\nevidence section as '(119 lines omitted)' + tail, hiding the integrity\nheader and the ENTIRE main.go diff. Second cycle: 144 lines omitted,\nidentical rejection. The tool-less reviewer correctly refused to approve\ninvisible evidence twice; planner@3 correctly escalated Blocked (cycle\nguard worked); seed fabro-8d26 remains open though code is verified.\n\n## Fix (workflow side, within our control)\n\nThe evidence capture must fit the RENDERED budget, not the raw output\nbudget: tail_lines appears to keep ~the last N lines and drop the head —\nexactly where our critical-first sections live.\n\n1. Measure the effective rendered cap empirically (prompt #3 evidence\n   section: ~119+visible lines -> derive chars).\n2. Rearchitect evidence.nu: the reviewer's verdict-critical material is\n   the DIFF — emit it FIRST inside command.output (tail_lines keeps the\n   tail... so instead: emit ONLY a compact integrity header + diff\n   tail-budgeted to the renderer's window, dropping our own nice-to-have\n   sections) OR move review to a node that renders full output.\n3. Engine-side (companion on denkhaus, update fabro-9467/fabro-e47c):\n   per-node preamble scoping lets the reviewer see ONLY the evidence —\n   no other stages, no renderer caps on the one input that matters.\n\n## Acceptance\n\n- a run whose seed-work diff is ~120 lines reaches the reviewer FULLY\n  rendered (no 'omitted' inside the evidence section)\n- reviewer approves a green-gate pass on visible evidence in one cycle\n- fabro-8d26 (verified correct) closes on the next run"}
 {"id":"fabro-meta-b3aa","title":"planner painpoint claims must be verified — deliver via context relay, not stated files","status":"open","type":"bug","priority":2,"createdAt":"2026-08-22T23:07:07.000Z","updatedAt":"2026-08-23T15:22:54.026Z","labels":["platform","painpoint-adopted"],"description":"Adopt from run 01M0NTS5XCXJ4V88M9MQWKD83G.\n\n## Failure\n\nPlanner@3's response claimed 'both painpoints are recorded in\n/workspace/fabro/.fabro/run-painpoints.jsonl and mirrored below' — the\ntool history shows only two `cat` reads (second: file missing), ZERO\nwrite calls. The staged file never existed; the refiner correctly\ndelivered nothing; the two genuinely valuable painpoints (evidence\ntruncation, deadlock diagnosis) were lost with only prose remnants in\nthe run log.\n\n## Fix (workflow side)\n\nThe prompt contract says 'append one JSON line to the staged file' —\nthe model claims the action without performing it (classic unverified\nclaim). Options:\n\n1. planner.md: mandate an explicit verify step — after writing, `cat`\n   the staged file and quote its line count in the response; the\n   reviewer-side check: response claims must cite file line counts.\n2. Stronger (engine, fabro-176b already filed): post-stage hook appends\n   declared painpoints deterministically — no LLM relay to trust.\n3. Short-term deterministic: a tiny pre-refiner command node that\n   extracts painpoints from context.workflow_painpoints into the staged\n   file (context is engine-verified, unlike claimed file writes).\n\nOption 3 removes the trust gap without engine changes: implement as\n`stage-painpoints.nu` between planner and refiner? Evaluate during\nimplementation; option 1 is the 5-minute stopgap.\n\n## Acceptance\n\n- a run ending with painpoint claims delivers matching rows to the\n  mailbox (verifiable in painpoints.jsonl)\n- planner responses that claim file writes without performing them are\n  caught (verify-step or deterministic relay)\n\n## LINK (2026-08-23): message-bus seed fabro-meta-8652\n\nThe trust failure documented here (planner claiming unwritten painpoint\nfiles) is failure class 1 of three relay failures; the message-bus\nconcept replaces the LLM relay entirely (message_send tool with\nengine-added sender provenance)."}
 {"id":"fabro-meta-e294","title":"develop: stale-base awareness after publish_blocked runs (unmerged run-branch remediation)","status":"open","type":"feature","priority":2,"createdAt":"2026-08-25T13:19:52.011Z","updatedAt":"2026-08-25T13:19:52.011Z","description":"## Context\n\nfabro-67e5 landed on the platform (denkhaus 7d4176995): a green graph whose\npublish failed now terminates as succeeded(publish_blocked) with a\nPublishFailed failure detail and remediation text; Slack sees run.completed\n(not run.failed). The workflow side (graph, prompts, notifications config)\nneeded no structural change — verified 2026-08-25 against meta/denkhaus-lab.\n\n## Problem\n\nWith auto_merge enabled, a publish_blocked run means NO PR was created (or\npush failed entirely), so the run branch fabro/run/<id> holds work that will\nnever merge back. The NEXT run clones the stale base:\n\n- the planner may re-implement work that already sits on an unmerged run\n  branch (its verification-only path only fires when acceptance criteria\n  happen to look satisfied in the stale worktree)\n- nothing in the loop (prepare, evidence, closeout) surfaces that the base\n  is behind unmerged run branches\n- the Slack remediation is the only signal; nobody acts on it autonomously\n\n## Candidate directions (decide together)\n\n1. prepare step: detect unmerged fabro/run/* branches ahead of base and\n   fail fast / annotate context (cheap git, deterministic)\n2. closeout: when tracker_empty routes to exit, check for unmerged run\n   branches and surface them in the terminal context\n3. evidence: include 'branches ahead of base' in the capture so the\n   reviewer/planner can see them\n4. accept as-is: Slack is the human signal; document the manual remediation\n   (open PR from fabro/run/<id>) as the run branch being the durability path\n\n## Acceptance\n\n- decision recorded (ADR or seed note) on which direction(s) ship\n- if 1-3 ship: deterministic, no LLM, tested against a run branch ahead\n  of base","labels":["platform","workflows","publish"]}
-{"id": "fabro-b158", "title": "Fix invalid reasoning_effort pin on planner node", "status": "open", "type": "task", "priority": 1, "createdAt": "2026-09-02T17:53:02.463Z", "updatedAt": "2026-09-03T17:28:47.338Z", "description": "In .fabro/workflows/develop/workflow.fabro (planner node, line 29) change reasoning_effort=\"medium\" to \"low\" or remove the node-level pin — glm-5.3 only allows low/high/max. Run 01M0QJ01P9E6EKX8S6TQN91G8E died 1.5s into the planner stage with invalid_request: model 'glm-5.3' does not support reasoning_effort 'medium' (0 tokens, will_retry=false), failing the run with 0 of 4 seeds touched. Expected effect: the planner's first LLM call succeeds and this exact failure class becomes impossible.", "labels": ["revision"]}
